Categorizing flakiness (very important in companies)

Teams classify flaky tests into 4 types:

1. ⏱ Timing issues
Element not ready
Page loads slowly
AJAX not finished
2. 🌐 Environment issues
Test server unstable
CI load issues
Network latency
3. 🔍 Selenium sync issues
NoSuchElementException
StaleElementReferenceException
4. 🧪 Test design issues
Poor assertions
Dependency between tests
Hard-coded data
🟡 3. Fix strategy (preferred over retry)
✔ A. Explicit waits (MOST IMPORTANT)

Instead of:

driver.find_element(By.ID, "login").click()

Companies enforce:

from selenium.webdriver.support.ui import WebDriverWait
from selenium.webdriver.support import expected_conditions as EC

WebDriverWait(driver, 10).until(
    EC.element_to_be_clickable((By.ID, "login"))
).click()
✔ B. Page Object Model (POM)

Reduces flaky locators and duplication.

class LoginPage:
    def login(self):
        self.wait_and_click(self.login_button)
✔ C. Stable locators strategy

Preferred order:

data-testid (BEST)
id
name
CSS selector
XPath (last resort)
🔴 4. Retry strategy (controlled, not blind)

Companies use smart retries only for known flaky categories

Install:
pip install pytest-rerunfailures
Example:
pytest --reruns 1 --reruns-delay 2
OR per test level (better control)
import pytest

@pytest.mark.flaky(reruns=2, reruns_delay=2)
def test_login():
    assert True
