3. What Each Folder/File Contains
tests/

Contains actual test cases.

Example:

def test_valid_login(login_page):
    login_page.login("admin", "password")
    assert login_page.is_login_successful()

Purpose:

Only business validations
No Selenium logic directly
Easy to read
pages/

Contains Page Object Model classes.

Example:

class LoginPage:

    def __init__(self, driver):
        self.driver = driver

    def enter_username(self, username):
        self.driver.find_element(*LoginLocators.USERNAME).send_keys(username)

    def login(self, user, pwd):
        self.enter_username(user)

Purpose:

Encapsulates page behavior
Keeps Selenium code centralized
Easier maintenance if UI changes
locators/

Stores element locators separately.

Example:

class LoginLocators:
    USERNAME = ("id", "username")
    PASSWORD = ("id", "password")
    LOGIN_BTN = ("xpath", "//button")

Purpose:

Centralized locator maintenance
Cleaner page classes
utilities/logger.py

Creates reusable logging setup.

Example:

import logging

def get_logger():
    logger = logging.getLogger()
    logger.setLevel(logging.INFO)

    fh = logging.FileHandler("logs/test.log")
    logger.addHandler(fh)

    return logger

Purpose:

Track execution
Debug failures
Audit execution flow
utilities/read_config.py

Reads configuration values.

Example:

from configparser import ConfigParser

config = ConfigParser()
config.read("config/config.ini")

base_url = config.get("settings", "base_url")

Purpose:

Environment independence
No hardcoded URLs/credentials
utilities/waits.py

Reusable wait methods.

Example:

WebDriverWait(driver, 10).until(
    EC.visibility_of_element_located(locator)
)

Purpose:

Avoid flaky tests
Synchronization handling
